1
00:00:16,957 --> 00:00:17,677
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a question.

2
00:00:18,138 --> 00:00:20,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Go ahead.

3
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:39,252
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about how to be self-reliant when there are many dhamma teachers all claiming, essentially, their own technique, view, process.

4
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:44,375
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the way that one can become self-reliant before right view?

5
00:00:47,448 --> 00:00:47,968
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

6
00:00:48,168 --> 00:00:49,949
[SPEAKER_05]: Self-reliance starts with the right view.

7
00:00:51,530 --> 00:00:55,112
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the... that's the peril of putridionous situation.

8
00:00:55,733 --> 00:00:56,013
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

9
00:00:57,714 --> 00:01:08,200
[SPEAKER_05]: So, yeah, that's why you have to be extra careful and extra careful, extra honest, extra transparent.

10
00:01:09,861 --> 00:01:13,243
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you're not... you're not acting out of mere preferences.

11
00:01:16,079 --> 00:01:33,071
[SPEAKER_05]: But actually, like, sound reasoning, using your intelligence to discern, and knowing that for as long as you are not self-reliant, well, you should not entirely rely on somebody else and take for granted.

12
00:01:33,271 --> 00:01:40,696
[SPEAKER_05]: But everything has to be investigated, in other words, with the goal of developing that self-reliance.

13
00:01:41,756 --> 00:01:41,976
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

14
00:01:43,827 --> 00:01:49,849
[SPEAKER_05]: Obviously, all of this needs to be done on the basis of keeping the precepts, sense restraint.

15
00:02:01,551 --> 00:02:07,793
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, how many so-called dhamma teachers actually have true self-reliance as a goal in their teaching?

16
00:02:14,632 --> 00:02:18,917
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, one of the criteria you can start seeing then, where does this teaching lead.

17
00:02:18,937 --> 00:02:24,282
[SPEAKER_00]: I have another question.

18
00:02:24,302 --> 00:02:25,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, go ahead.

19
00:02:26,665 --> 00:02:35,274
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the gradual training, all the way up to jhāna, the jhāna would be the right meditation.

20
00:02:37,224 --> 00:02:44,991
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that gradual training, it doesn't exactly express, he achieves right view.

21
00:02:45,832 --> 00:02:53,479
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the gradual training, it brings about jhāna, but it doesn't explicitly mention right view.

22
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:58,644
[SPEAKER_00]: But right view is a prerequisite for jhāna, as I understand.

23
00:02:58,864 --> 00:03:00,065
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, usually the gradual

24
00:03:01,068 --> 00:03:03,849
[SPEAKER_05]: Gradual training is taught to the monks.

25
00:03:04,269 --> 00:03:05,849
[SPEAKER_05]: They are the ones that undertook the training.

26
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:10,051
[SPEAKER_05]: So, generally, unless it's... it's the other way around.

27
00:03:10,351 --> 00:03:16,913
[SPEAKER_05]: When Buddha is talking to the monks, unless it's clearly stated that the monk does not have the right view, they would have the right view.

28
00:03:17,713 --> 00:03:25,035
[SPEAKER_05]: So, in all bhikkhus, and bhikkhus this, bhikkhus you should contemplate this, it's already presupposed that they understood what the practice is.

29
00:03:25,075 --> 00:03:30,597
[SPEAKER_05]: Otherwise, the Buddha wouldn't be talking on the level as if they already know what the practice is, and then encouraging them further.

30
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:38,433
[SPEAKER_05]: So, there were instances when some of the bhikkhus were not, didn't even have the right view, and those suttas, they would... it clearly states it.

31
00:03:38,573 --> 00:03:43,055
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, they would, usually, at the end of that sutta, get the right view, and so on.

32
00:03:43,735 --> 00:03:51,918
[SPEAKER_05]: So, eh... Having said that, you don't have... technically, you don't have to have the right view to have jhāna.

33
00:03:52,098 --> 00:03:57,160
[SPEAKER_05]: You just need to be thoroughly withdrawn, mind dried up from sensuality.

34
00:03:58,101 --> 00:04:00,622
[SPEAKER_05]: So, but again, any...

35
00:04:01,764 --> 00:04:10,432
[SPEAKER_05]: Any instruction, any notion, any contemplation that you might do that would result in that, today is known on account of the dhamma and the suttas.

36
00:04:11,172 --> 00:04:25,745
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it would be highly unlikely that you would be able to free your mind from sensuality, overcome it in jhāna, and not understand other much kind of lower aspects of the... from the suttas, such as, you know, Four Noble Truths and so on.

37
00:04:26,626 --> 00:04:29,228
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it would be... it would be unlikely that you wouldn't get the right view.

38
00:04:30,082 --> 00:04:33,188
[SPEAKER_05]: But, say, even again, theoretically, you still don't get the right view.

39
00:04:34,150 --> 00:04:40,662
[SPEAKER_05]: If you had developed jhānas, it means your mind would have already been pure from all those pollutions.

40
00:04:41,284 --> 00:04:41,684
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't know.

41
00:04:42,378 --> 00:04:50,564
[SPEAKER_05]: 95 percent, let's say, which means it would be much more likely, much easier to then get the right view, because there are less obstructions of obscuring it.

42
00:04:51,365 --> 00:04:56,609
[SPEAKER_05]: There's a difference when the Buddha would say, some person has a lot of dust in their eyes, some person doesn't.

43
00:04:57,049 --> 00:05:08,938
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, some some some person's mind is much more developed, someone else's isn't, and they would need much more, like, instruction, repeated, and so on, and would take a lot longer for them to

44
00:05:09,778 --> 00:05:10,539
[SPEAKER_05]: Together, I view.

45
00:05:10,919 --> 00:05:19,264
[SPEAKER_05]: But if the mind is developed from the things that are obscuring this understanding, then, if you turn it directly towards the understanding, it won't take that much.

46
00:05:19,644 --> 00:05:21,625
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, as I said, there's not much standing in the way.

47
00:05:23,947 --> 00:05:35,254
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you don't need to

48
00:05:36,322 --> 00:05:40,485
[SPEAKER_02]: Wait to have the right view before starting the gradual training, as far as I understand.

49
00:05:40,505 --> 00:05:40,985
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

50
00:05:41,205 --> 00:05:51,052
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah, that's... you know, you... that... but by doing it, you... you... you actually have a chance to understand it, for what it is, and by doing so, you get the right view.

51
00:05:52,593 --> 00:05:54,915
[SPEAKER_05]: But as long as that doesn't mean that

52
00:05:56,876 --> 00:06:00,017
[SPEAKER_05]: People should be doing a technique that they think is the gradual training.

53
00:06:00,037 --> 00:06:01,957
[SPEAKER_05]: They will then bring the pleasure of jhāna.

54
00:06:02,297 --> 00:06:05,638
[SPEAKER_05]: They will then result in the right view, because that's seriously wrong view.

55
00:06:06,578 --> 00:06:19,201
[SPEAKER_05]: So, in the process of doing the gradual training, you need to be uprooting your wrong views about where the practice is, the place of sensory strength and withdrawal, that self-transparency.

56
00:06:19,781 --> 00:06:26,222
[SPEAKER_05]: So... And that's what I'm saying, like, if you start keeping the precepts rightly, not relying on

57
00:06:27,179 --> 00:06:52,533
[SPEAKER_05]: The authority of a teacher, not relying even on a community, in that sense, or a tradition, but doing it internally, as your... as solely dependent on your determination, your upholding of that direction, it would be highly unlikely that you would remain non-transparent about your motives and expectations and other kind of views you might have.

58
00:06:53,357 --> 00:07:05,641
[SPEAKER_05]: But usually, as I said, when people start their graduate training, they would take on the precepts on account of one of these factors, so that they don't have to think for themselves, or they don't have to doubt it, they don't have to second-guess it every day.

59
00:07:06,021 --> 00:07:07,361
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's like, no, you do have to.

60
00:07:07,541 --> 00:07:08,501
[SPEAKER_05]: That's kind of the point.

61
00:07:08,702 --> 00:07:13,143
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how you realize the extent of the hindrances, of the doubt, of the mind, and so on.

62
00:07:14,952 --> 00:07:22,117
[SPEAKER_05]: So, understanding how to do the gradual training rightly, see, that's already something that can be developed in people.

63
00:07:22,537 --> 00:07:32,184
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, it's not just you read these steps and do them, because each and one of those steps, there are layers you can peel off of until you do it rightly.

64
00:07:32,224 --> 00:07:34,946
[SPEAKER_05]: In the beginning, it won't be correct, for sure, but...

65
00:07:35,446 --> 00:07:42,871
[SPEAKER_05]: So, yeah, it doesn't mean you shouldn't try anything until you get the right view, but you need to recognize that whatever you try,

66
00:07:43,678 --> 00:07:47,621
[SPEAKER_05]: Without the right view, well, it won't be right, for whatever reason.

67
00:07:49,563 --> 00:08:06,576
[SPEAKER_00]: When I'm practicing, like, restraint, just in regards to non-activity, per se, meaning not trying to do anything, particularly out of any hindrance that might happen,

68
00:08:08,597 --> 00:08:19,186
[SPEAKER_00]: I get maybe a point where it feels like any investigation I do is out of the desire to get rid of the doubt about it.

69
00:08:19,867 --> 00:08:22,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it just feels more pleasant to not think about it.

70
00:08:23,690 --> 00:08:24,430
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, yeah.

71
00:08:24,491 --> 00:08:29,254
[SPEAKER_05]: So, as I said, it's not like something you can just stick to one

72
00:08:30,152 --> 00:08:31,033
[SPEAKER_05]: Just stick to one way.

73
00:08:31,053 --> 00:08:32,834
[SPEAKER_05]: So, never, never, never act out.

74
00:08:32,894 --> 00:08:33,435
[SPEAKER_05]: Never do this.

75
00:08:33,495 --> 00:08:35,977
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, again, it depends on why you're doing it at a time.

76
00:08:36,678 --> 00:08:38,459
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if it's too tight, you need to loosen it.

77
00:08:38,499 --> 00:08:40,001
[SPEAKER_05]: If it's too loose, you need to tighten it.

78
00:08:40,501 --> 00:08:47,067
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if you are actually preferring the pleasure of non-investigation, that's probably a good sign that you should start investigating.

79
00:08:49,592 --> 00:08:52,575
[SPEAKER_05]: And even if your mind is coming out of desire, it doesn't matter.

80
00:08:53,055 --> 00:08:53,435
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not.

81
00:08:54,416 --> 00:08:56,138
[SPEAKER_05]: Because otherwise it would be pleasant.

82
00:08:56,158 --> 00:09:00,302
[SPEAKER_05]: So you realize desire is where the pleasure is.

83
00:09:02,604 --> 00:09:12,333
[SPEAKER_04]: Perhaps another factor in questioning the authenticity of a teacher or a teaching is

84
00:09:13,287 --> 00:09:18,893
[SPEAKER_04]: To find out how prepared the teacher is to having discussions.

85
00:09:19,894 --> 00:09:29,784
[SPEAKER_04]: Because somebody might say, no, this is my practice, this is my method, it's working for me, and based on that, they basically block everything else.

86
00:09:30,305 --> 00:09:34,769
[SPEAKER_04]: But if there's no attitude to even be open to alternative views,

87
00:09:35,630 --> 00:09:38,412
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's the... yeah.

88
00:09:38,512 --> 00:09:43,416
[SPEAKER_04]: People can be open to views, but no, no, no.

89
00:09:43,797 --> 00:09:45,758
[SPEAKER_05]: Can you actually reason your position?

90
00:09:46,359 --> 00:09:47,159
[SPEAKER_05]: Give arguments.

91
00:09:47,860 --> 00:09:49,881
[SPEAKER_05]: Not just, like, I'm not hearing any of this.

92
00:09:50,122 --> 00:09:51,022
[SPEAKER_05]: This is the way I did it.

93
00:09:51,042 --> 00:09:51,583
[SPEAKER_05]: That's it.

94
00:09:51,643 --> 00:09:52,403
[SPEAKER_05]: Finished story.

95
00:09:52,844 --> 00:09:53,064
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

96
00:09:53,084 --> 00:09:55,326
[SPEAKER_05]: So, like, can... can you ask questions?

97
00:09:55,906 --> 00:09:58,688
[SPEAKER_05]: Can you get the corresponding answer to what you asked?

98
00:09:58,808 --> 00:10:06,213
[SPEAKER_05]: Not like a generic, whatever you asked, you just get, well, watch your nostrils, or chant this mantra, or let go.

99
00:10:06,834 --> 00:10:07,974
[SPEAKER_05]: Every, everything, everything.

100
00:10:07,994 --> 00:10:09,896
[SPEAKER_05]: Whoever comes, whichever does, it's just the same thing.

101
00:10:09,936 --> 00:10:10,556
[SPEAKER_05]: Just let go.

102
00:10:10,656 --> 00:10:12,577
[SPEAKER_05]: Just, just, yeah, just chant.

103
00:10:12,618 --> 00:10:13,778
[SPEAKER_05]: Just, just sit cross-legged.

104
00:10:14,339 --> 00:10:20,163
[SPEAKER_05]: That's, again, if, if, if that were the way out, well, surely we would have come across it in the suttas as, as a way out.

105
00:10:20,943 --> 00:10:26,047
[SPEAKER_05]: But it was, like, quite... much more elaborate, much more layered, as I said before.

106
00:10:26,787 --> 00:10:42,417
[SPEAKER_05]: So, eh... there is... there is definitely, actually, a factor you can... you can see, even on the basis of, eh... of someone else's instruction, whether it is just an enclosed kind of faith-based, just do this, or there is... there is some...

107
00:10:43,578 --> 00:10:54,942
[SPEAKER_05]: Intellect behind it, so to speak, there is some reason in there, clear kind of reflection, that can then be, you know, shared, questioned, and so on, explained differently, and so on.

108
00:10:55,142 --> 00:10:55,362
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

109
00:10:57,223 --> 00:10:59,084
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's definitely important, actually.

110
00:11:01,725 --> 00:11:10,028
[SPEAKER_02]: In the suttas, you know, whoever will come to the Buddha with a question, sometimes even quite antagonistic, wanting to debase,

111
00:11:11,072 --> 00:11:15,015
[SPEAKER_02]: He would never, would always be able to answer every question.

112
00:11:15,375 --> 00:11:16,116
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

113
00:11:16,136 --> 00:11:20,039
[SPEAKER_02]: It was, you know, doesn't matter who, from what direction.

114
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:28,045
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, apart those questions he refused to answer, but obviously, then he would give reasons for refusing to answer those questions.

115
00:11:28,085 --> 00:11:28,806
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like...

116
00:11:28,826 --> 00:11:30,828
[SPEAKER_05]: The ones that are based on the wrong premise.

117
00:11:34,287 --> 00:11:37,129
[SPEAKER_04]: And, sorry, and also the seclusion is a factor.

118
00:11:37,729 --> 00:11:43,833
[SPEAKER_04]: There's this discourse where somebody approaches the Buddha and says, well, we have all these different wanderers and they have all these views.

119
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:45,841
[SPEAKER_04]: How can you now know which one is right?

120
00:11:46,341 --> 00:11:55,107
[SPEAKER_04]: And then he does say, okay, one factor is, do these people actually practically delight in seclusion and not just hang out with other people the whole time?

121
00:11:55,608 --> 00:11:56,488
[SPEAKER_04]: That's now another thing.

122
00:11:57,209 --> 00:12:10,578
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's basically even regardless of... this might be a sort of lesser standard, but still, because of that factor is not there, it's very questionable whether there's sufficient strength in the mind to have the right kind of discernment.

123
00:12:11,438 --> 00:12:12,619
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah.

124
00:12:12,939 --> 00:12:14,760
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's another factor.

125
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:20,662
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd say, maybe it's another factor thing to be aware of in this.

126
00:12:21,823 --> 00:12:27,205
[SPEAKER_02]: Things that it's not... it's not necessarily just about, like,

127
00:12:29,388 --> 00:12:42,435
[SPEAKER_02]: Choosing a particular teacher, you then say, oh yeah, now that's the one that I've settled on, and I'm fairly sure that that's... because that would be obviously making yourself dependent on this.

128
00:12:42,855 --> 00:12:58,543
[SPEAKER_02]: I think you need to kind of keep in mind that the only way, really, that you'll have a criteria to know which teacher, if there even is one, for example, like you don't even need to take for granted that there is even one teacher out there who is

129
00:12:59,375 --> 00:13:00,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Teaching the same thing.

130
00:13:00,215 --> 00:13:04,938
[SPEAKER_02]: What needs to be the priority is that you yourself know what's right and what's wrong for yourself.

131
00:13:05,318 --> 00:13:09,600
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, from that perspective, you would be able to be sure.

132
00:13:10,261 --> 00:13:19,025
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, this teaching, I'm sure, because I've really trained in it and I've verified it for myself, that I can see.

133
00:13:20,246 --> 00:13:22,027
[SPEAKER_05]: What about if somebody can't help?

134
00:13:23,996 --> 00:13:27,638
[SPEAKER_05]: They just can't help but say, well, this is my teacher.

135
00:13:27,798 --> 00:13:28,599
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm so inspired.

136
00:13:28,679 --> 00:13:29,239
[SPEAKER_05]: What do they do?

137
00:13:30,139 --> 00:13:32,020
[SPEAKER_05]: Do they go and find another one or what do they do?

138
00:13:32,201 --> 00:13:33,121
[SPEAKER_05]: How do they deal with that?

139
00:13:36,563 --> 00:13:39,805
[SPEAKER_05]: Because everything that this person says, everything just resonates.

140
00:13:40,685 --> 00:13:44,527
[SPEAKER_05]: He's the right teacher and that's it.

141
00:13:44,587 --> 00:13:45,608
[SPEAKER_05]: But, again,

142
00:13:47,078 --> 00:13:50,599
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't know that, so what do you do in that occasion?

143
00:13:50,639 --> 00:13:53,800
[SPEAKER_05]: How can you know whether your inspiration is right or wrong?

144
00:13:53,960 --> 00:13:59,122
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, you're factually inspired, you're factually uplifted, you factually find sense in what's being said.

145
00:13:59,662 --> 00:14:02,763
[SPEAKER_05]: You just stop caring about anybody else, any other instruction.

146
00:14:04,063 --> 00:14:06,384
[SPEAKER_05]: But how do you now not blind yourself with that?

147
00:14:06,904 --> 00:14:07,444
[SPEAKER_05]: What do you do?

148
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:19,702
[SPEAKER_03]: Take the medicine from the doctor.

149
00:14:19,902 --> 00:14:20,223
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

150
00:14:20,503 --> 00:14:22,665
[SPEAKER_03]: And, yeah, take it on faith.

151
00:14:23,366 --> 00:14:24,086
[SPEAKER_03]: You have to actually...

152
00:14:24,987 --> 00:14:25,528
[SPEAKER_03]: Apply it.

153
00:14:25,588 --> 00:14:26,929
[SPEAKER_03]: Apply it in the end.

154
00:14:27,169 --> 00:14:28,771
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, okay, the application.

155
00:14:29,031 --> 00:14:29,732
[SPEAKER_03]: But, yeah, that's...

156
00:14:33,477 --> 00:14:37,161
[SPEAKER_03]: And recognize this blind faith, I mean, as a hindrance?

157
00:14:37,201 --> 00:14:41,606
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, yeah, maybe not as a hindrance, but recognize it as a factor there, and then question that.

158
00:14:41,746 --> 00:14:47,932
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, so you don't have to deny it, or I must find another one, or I must undo the inspiration, but it's like, what is it that I'm finding inspiring?

159
00:14:48,653 --> 00:14:50,395
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it stuff that is genuinely inspiring?

160
00:14:55,816 --> 00:14:58,138
[SPEAKER_05]: You find, yeah, that's the right guy or something.

161
00:14:58,699 --> 00:15:00,480
[SPEAKER_05]: But, or is it... no, no, no.

162
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:06,806
[SPEAKER_05]: Factually, on the basis of my previous practice and everything else, things that I said, that's what you find inspiring.

163
00:15:06,866 --> 00:15:11,390
[SPEAKER_05]: Things that you're required to do as a practice, that's what you find inspiring, and so on.

164
00:15:11,671 --> 00:15:12,211
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, okay.

165
00:15:12,251 --> 00:15:17,396
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I was... if it's... if it's that, as in, if it's genuinely things that

166
00:15:18,959 --> 00:15:32,890
[SPEAKER_02]: If you have made you understood things that you didn't hear from anybody else, and that have actually made you see things in a different way, not just like, oh, this clicks with what I was already thinking, sort of thing.

167
00:15:34,171 --> 00:15:37,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, yeah, that's a kind of a fairly good criteria.

168
00:15:37,713 --> 00:15:42,197
[SPEAKER_02]: But even then, I would say that it's still not... Yeah, you still have to do it.

169
00:15:42,257 --> 00:15:43,157
[SPEAKER_05]: You still have to do it.

170
00:15:43,177 --> 00:15:43,898
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah.

171
00:15:43,918 --> 00:15:47,361
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's like, so, you might have inspiration, and then you start questioning that.

172
00:15:48,641 --> 00:15:52,909
[SPEAKER_05]: As to what is the basis for it, what's your motivation behind.

173
00:15:53,330 --> 00:15:55,533
[SPEAKER_05]: See if you're afraid to question.

174
00:15:57,265 --> 00:15:58,365
[SPEAKER_05]: What that teacher says.

175
00:15:58,625 --> 00:16:02,406
[SPEAKER_05]: Not necessarily with him, if he's on the other side of the world, but with yourself.

176
00:16:03,327 --> 00:16:05,747
