342
00:33:10,477 --> 00:33:12,959
[SPEAKER_05]: The ugly wants you to see it as beautiful, and so on.

343
00:33:13,559 --> 00:33:21,225
[SPEAKER_05]: There was at the Rathapala Sutta in Majjhima Nikaya where, you know, he was already, if not fully enlightened, he was already way beyond sensuality.

344
00:33:21,526 --> 00:33:29,412
[SPEAKER_05]: When he went home and the father tried to entice him first with the wealth, got his wives to try and seduce him, and he says, stop harassing me.

345
00:33:29,733 --> 00:33:31,174
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, all this is harassment.

346
00:33:31,934 --> 00:33:43,702
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's not that he was like oblivious to what was happening, the significance of those acts, of seductive behavior, of the value of gold and so on, but he could not not see it for what it is.

347
00:33:43,822 --> 00:33:56,390
[SPEAKER_05]: So, he told father off, told him to better to put a gold at the bottom of the river, because it would bring less suffering their way, and called his wives sisters, and they kind of got a bit upset and fainted or something.

348
00:33:57,722 --> 00:34:05,384
[SPEAKER_05]: But, yeah, he was factually, like, I don't want to say annoyed in the terms of angry, but, yeah, his senses were being bombarded the same.

349
00:34:05,404 --> 00:34:13,207
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why anāgāmī-ārahant would not be able to live... Well, we had anāgāmīs that lived household lives, but anāgāmī-ārahant would not be able to live a household life.

350
00:34:14,247 --> 00:34:21,269
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, simply, why would he bear annoyance and harassment of the senses that have no desire towards those things?

351
00:34:21,929 --> 00:34:23,289
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's just discomfort, basically.

352
00:34:23,610 --> 00:34:25,050
[SPEAKER_05]: It's factually uncomfortable.

353
00:34:26,367 --> 00:34:27,427
[SPEAKER_05]: That's exactly the bait.

354
00:34:27,447 --> 00:34:28,728
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's the progress.

355
00:34:28,848 --> 00:34:33,589
[SPEAKER_05]: Not going in the direction of the bait, regardless of the discomfort.

356
00:34:55,241 --> 00:34:59,362
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, yeah, sometimes you just have to avoid this conflict.

357
00:34:59,782 --> 00:35:04,203
[SPEAKER_05]: If it's too much pressure, it's better to upgrade the environment where you won't be as pressured.

358
00:35:04,904 --> 00:35:07,684
[SPEAKER_05]: But, yeah, then don't confuse that for progress.

359
00:35:08,304 --> 00:35:18,207
[SPEAKER_05]: You still recognize that, well, but have I seen the peril, the danger, and, you know, vomited the kind of the desire out and so on, or am I just sheltered here?

360
00:35:22,210 --> 00:35:27,132
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's something, if you're transparent, you know the answer, even without having to go back to the environment, they will provoke you.

361
00:35:33,173 --> 00:35:34,534
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the bait is the pressure.

362
00:35:36,775 --> 00:35:40,596
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, the bait is the pleasure that you get if you give in to the pressure.

363
00:35:43,297 --> 00:35:45,477
[SPEAKER_05]: The bait is hidden in the pleasure, let's say it like that.

364
00:35:49,078 --> 00:35:50,699
[SPEAKER_05]: So, acting out towards it means...

365
00:35:53,226 --> 00:35:55,730
[SPEAKER_03]: Biting, taking, taking up the bit.

366
00:35:56,131 --> 00:35:58,114
[SPEAKER_03]: It's the gratification, the danger.

367
00:35:58,855 --> 00:36:01,339
[SPEAKER_03]: And it also could be the escape, when you understand it.

368
00:36:01,619 --> 00:36:06,767
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it's like putting honey on a razor blade.

369
00:36:07,068 --> 00:36:07,288
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

370
00:36:07,749 --> 00:36:08,470
[SPEAKER_04]: And then making it.

371
00:36:13,290 --> 00:36:15,933
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, the poisonous drink, the simile of the sutras, eh?

372
00:36:16,653 --> 00:36:22,319
[SPEAKER_05]: That's the... the subtlety of sensuality is the... it's the subtlety of debate.

373
00:36:22,399 --> 00:36:23,300
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't really see it.

374
00:36:23,600 --> 00:36:25,082
[SPEAKER_05]: Your senses do not perceive.

375
00:36:25,422 --> 00:36:31,668
[SPEAKER_05]: Your eyes, ears, they don't see anything that's disagreeable and so on.

376
00:36:32,109 --> 00:36:33,911
[SPEAKER_05]: But the mind is factually pressured by it.

377
00:36:34,371 --> 00:36:36,774
[SPEAKER_05]: And if you, on that level, you discern debate.

378
00:36:37,672 --> 00:36:43,653
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, you know that there is a poison in a drink that is otherwise undetectable by your senses.

379
00:36:43,753 --> 00:36:49,015
[SPEAKER_05]: But now, the knowledge that it is poisonous, that... that cannot change.

380
00:36:49,035 --> 00:36:55,896
[SPEAKER_05]: So, although you can't smell it, you can't smell the poison, you can't touch it, you can't see it, but you can know that it's there.

381
00:36:57,197 --> 00:37:00,618
[SPEAKER_05]: And when you know that it's there, no amount of pressure will make you take that drink.

382
00:37:01,698 --> 00:37:06,039
[SPEAKER_05]: When you fully discern that it is there, actually, you see it on that mental level.

383
00:37:07,447 --> 00:37:15,049
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, as we said before, if it weren't, why does sensuality need to pressure you towards acting?

384
00:37:15,349 --> 00:37:22,891
[SPEAKER_05]: If it's wholesome, if it's good, why does it need to bombard you and trick you and manipulate you and control you?

385
00:37:23,551 --> 00:37:25,191
[SPEAKER_05]: Why does it have a nature of a trap?

386
00:37:25,511 --> 00:37:31,513
[SPEAKER_05]: Why does, you know, like, if it's generally good, why is it set up in a way that I end up being trapped?

387
00:37:32,856 --> 00:37:33,797
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's the whole point.

388
00:37:33,877 --> 00:37:37,101
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, really, you could recognize the pattern and you see, yeah, so this...

389
00:37:37,301 --> 00:37:42,326
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't know the implications of this setup, but I know that it cannot be for my welfare.

390
00:37:43,267 --> 00:37:49,614
[SPEAKER_05]: As the suttas would say, he knows that, like, a hunter does not set up a trap for a welfare of the deer.

391
00:37:50,715 --> 00:37:54,780
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the monk reflects that anything that has a nature of a trap, hey, look,

392
00:37:56,067 --> 00:37:57,228
[SPEAKER_05]: It cannot be for my welfare.

393
00:37:57,388 --> 00:37:59,729
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't know who set it up, I don't know why, and I don't need to know.

394
00:37:59,850 --> 00:38:03,852
[SPEAKER_05]: All I know is how to reckon as a trap when I see it, and not fall into it.

395
00:38:05,934 --> 00:38:13,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of difficult to try to convince someone who is just... No, it's pointless.

396
00:38:13,899 --> 00:38:16,161
[SPEAKER_05]: And I think you should try to convince people about it.

397
00:38:17,141 --> 00:38:19,423
[SPEAKER_05]: You're devaluing the actual practice.

398
00:38:21,184 --> 00:38:25,207
[SPEAKER_05]: If somebody needs convincing, they're not going to make the effort necessary for understanding.

399
00:38:26,175 --> 00:38:28,437
[SPEAKER_05]: You need to meet the instruction halfway.

400
00:38:29,757 --> 00:38:37,062
[SPEAKER_05]: If it's shoved down your throat and put on you, it's not... it's going to be seen as something completely different from what it actually is.

401
00:38:37,082 --> 00:38:41,105
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's not obvious for everybody that sensuality is a danger.

402
00:38:41,686 --> 00:38:42,906
[SPEAKER_05]: No, of course not.

403
00:38:46,609 --> 00:38:51,572
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, if it were obvious, we wouldn't need all the instruction to free ourselves from it.

